<li class="font-claude-response-body whitespace-normal break-words pl-2" data-sourcepos="31:1-31:29;1186-1214">Cross-Site Scripting (XSS)</li>
<li class="font-claude-response-body whitespace-normal break-words pl-2" data-sourcepos="32:1-32:43;1215-1257">Insecure Direct Object References (IDOR)</li>
<li class="font-claude-response-body whitespace-normal break-words pl-2" data-sourcepos="33:1-33:16;1258-1273">SQL Injection</li>
<li class="font-claude-response-body whitespace-normal break-words pl-2" data-sourcepos="34:1-34:18;1274-1291">Race Conditions</li>
</ul>

<hr class="border-border-200 border-t-0.5 my-3 mx-1.5">
<h2 dir="ltr" class="mt-3 -mb-1 text-[1.125rem] font-bold break-words" data-sourcepos="38:1-38:36;1298-1333">2. Five Vulnerable Code Patterns</h2>

<p class="font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal" dir="ltr" data-sourcepos="40:1-40:348;1335-1682">Each example below looks like ordinary, functional code — none of it raises obvious red flags on a quick read, and all of it passes a basic "does it work" test. That's exactly why these patterns are dangerous: authorization, sanitization, and trust-boundary checks are easy to omit when the focus is on shipping a feature rather than defending it.</p>

<h3 dir="ltr" class="mt-2 -mb-1 text-base font-bold break-words" data-sourcepos="42:1-42:41;1684-1724">2.1 IDOR / BOLA — Order Cancellation</h3>

<p class="font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal" dir="ltr" data-sourcepos="44:1-44:29;1726-1754"><strong>Stack:</strong> Node.js / Express</p>

